Acute Sinusitis
Opthalmology & ENT
Facial pain/pressure, Purulent nasal discharge, Pain worse bending forward

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OET-002
Reason for Visit: Unrelenting facial pain and high fever not responding to over-the-counter analgesia.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified severe unilateral facial pain, high fever persisting beyond ten days, and localized pressure raising concern for orbital or intracranial complications of acute sinusitis. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to rule out cellulitis or abscess formation and commence immediate targeted pharmacotherapy, preventing deterioration before routine GP availability.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My face feels like it is going to explode when I bend over, and the pain in my teeth is unbearable."
History of Present Illness: Condition evolved from a presumed viral upper respiratory tract infection two weeks prior. Currently reports unilateral facial pain, pressure, and fullness localized over the maxillary and frontal sinuses. Pain severely worsens when bending forward. Notes thick, purulent, green nasal discharge, halitosis, and persistent post-nasal drip causing a nocturnal cough. Reports decreased sense of smell and a maxillary toothache. Fever has persisted for 12 days.
Functional Status: Independent but currently unable to sleep or work due to pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Lives alone, sleep deprivation affecting cognitive baseline.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/80 | HR: 92 | Temp: 38.2 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Visibly exhausted, holding right side of face.
Physical Exam: Exquisite tenderness to direct palpation and percussion over the right maxillary and frontal sinuses. Purulent green discharge visible in the right middle meatus on anterior rhinoscopy. Oropharynx clear, no dental caries visualized, but percussion of right upper molars elicits referred sinus pain. Neurological and cranial nerve exams intact. Normal extraocular movements; no periorbital edema or erythema.
Environmental Observations: Multiple empty packets of pseudoephedrine and paracetamol on the bedside table.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Discontinued OTC decongestants due to rebound risk.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Bacterial Sinusitis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Periorbital Cellulitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of periorbital erythema, edema, and normal extraocular movements without proptosis.

Dental Abscess - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Lack of focal gingival swelling or severe localized dental caries; pain is generalized to the sinus floor.

Trigeminal Neuralgia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Presence of purulent nasal discharge, fever, and continuous rather than sharp, electric-shock type pain.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (living alone, exhausted)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

Patient exhibits classic symptoms of acute bacterial rhinosinusitis with severe pain, fever persisting >10 days, and purulent discharge. The extreme severity of the localized facial pain and prolonged fever required immediate face-to-face assessment to exclude dangerous contiguous spread (orbital cellulitis or meningitis) and to provide definitive prescription analgesia and antibiotics. Delaying this assessment would have left the patient at unacceptable risk of acute neurological/orbital complications and severe unmanaged pain throughout the after-hours period.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Oral Amoxicillin/Clavulanate 875/125mg BD for 7 days. Intranasal Corticosteroid (Mometasone) 2 sprays per nostril daily. Prescribed Oral Tramadol 50mg PRN for severe breakthrough pain.
Positive Behavioral Change: To reduce nasal congestion naturally, use a saline sinus rinse twice daily before the steroid spray. The immediate clearance of mucus provides positive reinforcement (easier breathing), ensuring compliance with the topical steroid that requires a clean mucosa to function effectively.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 5 days to assess resolution.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if patient develops swelling or redness around the eyes, double vision, severe escalating headache, stiff neck, or altered mental status.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical; assumes uncomplicated sinus infection.
Clinical Course: Antibiotics and steroids should reduce pain and discharge within 48-72 hours.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency care if you experience changes to your vision, swelling around your eyes, or a stiff neck with severe headache, as these suggest the infection is spreading.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The antibiotic targets the bacterial infection trapped in the sinus cavity. The nasal steroid reduces the internal swelling to allow the sinus to drain.
Actionable Steps: Perform saline sinus rinses prior to nasal steroid administration. Sleep with head elevated on two pillows to utilize gravity for drainage.

